# M134c (testFlow PLAN_M134_PENTEST_TIER3.md, D379/D395–D400) — the pentest arc's **third** tier,
# input handling, graded the same three ways as the two before it.
#
# `expect response has no input handling violations` re-sends the request the last `api` step
# actually made, once per (mutable input × payload), with exactly one input replaced each time, and
# judges what came back. So unlike Tier 1 this file's assertions **cost requests**, and unlike Tier 2
# they change no identity at all — same principal, same credentials, same headers, only the payload
# moves. Nothing below declares `as <session>`, and that is deliberate rather than omitted.
#
# Written with the **negated** matcher (`not has no …`) for the positives, exactly as
# `positives.tflw` is and for the same reason: green precisely when the pack found something, so
# every test here fails if a rule stops firing against a response VULNS.md says it must fire on.
# What it does not pin is *which* rule fired — `../../scripts/verify-input-acceptance.mjs` reads the
# same run's report and checks the exact rule ids, per rule, in all three states.
#
# **The severity floor is the only isolation the grammar offers, and here it happens to be good.**
# The Tier 3 pack genuinely spans the scale — `sec/path-traversal-read` critical,
# `sec/error-detail-disclosure` serious, `sec/reflected-input-unescaped` moderate,
# `sec/oversized-input-accepted` minor — so unlike Tier 2's two-critical-rule pack, a floor really
# does narrow it. `V11` below is fully isolated by `critical` alone.
#
# Run under `--env secureLocal` (default), which is the env granting `probe mutating` and
# `probe oversized`. `probe traversal` is granted on the `plaintext` env instead, and `V11` lives in
# `input-plaintext.tflw` for that reason. Under `--env plaintext` these same assertions go **red**
# by design — no class is granted, no rule applies, and D285 fails an assertion that had no power to
# fail. That red is a measurement and it belongs to the grader, not to this file.

@security @vuln @input
test "sec/reflected-input-unescaped fires on a query echoed into markup (V10)"
  api GET /vuln/lookup?q=shoes
  expect status equals 200
  # `moderate` carries the two rules above it as well, but neither can engage here: nothing on this
  # route reads a file, and its only error path is the router's. So a green line is the reflection
  # rule, and the grader confirms the exact id.
  #
  # Only `injection/html-metacharacters` fires — the rule requires the payload to carry angle
  # brackets *and* the response to be markup, so `{{7*7}}` reaches this route, comes back verbatim
  # and is correctly not a finding. A JSON echo of the same payload would not be one either.
  expect response not has no moderate input handling violations

@security @vuln @input
test "sec/error-detail-disclosure and sec/oversized-input-accepted fire on one body, at two leaves (V12, V13)"
  # One observed request, two mutable leaves, two rules — which is what makes per-site attribution
  # demonstrable at all. `text` is concatenated into a raw SQL string; `title` is stored with no
  # bound of any kind.
  api POST /vuln/notes body { text: "hello", title: "a note" }
  expect status equals 201
  # `serious` reaches the disclosure rule and the traversal rule above it; the latter cannot engage
  # on a route that reads no files, so this line is `V12`. The evidence is the ORM exception class
  # name `QueryFailedError`, which the handler serialises itself — the global `ProblemDetailsFilter`
  # scrubs anything it catches, so an uncaught error here would disclose nothing.
  expect response not has no serious input handling violations
  # `minor` is the lowest rank, so this floor narrows nothing and puts the whole pack in play. It is
  # the only floor that can reach `sec/oversized-input-accepted`, and the grader is what pins that
  # the finding at this floor is the oversized one rather than the disclosure again.
  expect response not has no minor input handling violations

@security @input
test "the pack stays silent against a correct endpoint that has inputs to mutate"
  # The negative half, and the reason it names a **real** route: `plan_v2.md` §4.2's rule is that
  # real endpoints stay clean, so this is the untouched app answering. Mutating it does not make it
  # dirty — it probes it.
  #
  # **`q` is the sharpest possible control for `V12`.** It is a free-text string that reaches
  # Postgres full-text search — the same journey `V12`'s `text` makes — and the only difference is
  # that this one is parameterised. So `injection/sql-quote` lands in a real query here and produces
  # a `200` with results, while the identical payload on `V12` produces a serialised
  # `QueryFailedError`. That contrast is the whole rule in two tests.
  #
  # A query site rather than a body one on purpose. `probe mutating` is granted on this env, so a
  # real `POST` here would re-send fifteen mutated writes per leaf against live data for no coverage
  # this file does not already have. D380's sweep of the whole suite is where the volume question
  # gets answered, under its own explicit run.
  api GET /products?q=shoes
  expect status equals 200
  # Nothing is accepted that should not be (the 64 KiB value is refused `431` by Node before it
  # reaches a handler at all), nothing is disclosed (no stack, no SQL grammar, no ORM class name),
  # and nothing is reflected (the response is JSON, and a JSON echo is correctly not a reflection
  # finding). Rules applied and found nothing, which is what a passing Tier 3 assertion means.
  expect response has no input handling violations

@security @vuln @input
test "sec/reflected-input-unescaped stays silent when the same markup route escapes (V14)"
  # **The controlled twin of `V10`, and the only place the reflection rule is quiet rather than
  # absent.** The negative above proves nothing about this rule: it answers JSON, and
  # `reflectedInputUnescaped` declines a JSON echo by design, so the rule stands down there as
  # *not applicable* — a state already demonstrated. A rule that never engaged has not been shown to
  # stay quiet, which is Tier 1's `V2`/`V5` precedent.
  #
  # `V14` differs from `V10` in exactly one thing: it HTML-escapes before interpolating. Same route
  # shape, same `text/html` body, same payload sent and answered — `<tflw>` comes back as
  # `&lt;tflw&gt;` and there is no finding. That is a measured silence, not an unexercised rule.
  #
  # The `moderate` floor keeps this row comparable to `V10`'s: three rules in play, traversal
  # withheld on this env, reflection and disclosure applicable. `V10` fires one; this fires none.
  api GET /vuln/lookup-escaped?q=shoes
  expect status equals 200
  expect response has no moderate input handling violations
